WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:06.309
Every now and then I hear stories about people
who can perform amazing feats of mathematics,

00:00:06.309 --> 00:00:08.210
often in their head.

00:00:08.210 --> 00:00:14.710
And through comments on my videos I've learned
about one such person named Shakuntala Devi,

00:00:14.710 --> 00:00:18.160
also known as The Human Computer.

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:24.529
She holds the world record for the fastest
human computation which she gained in 1980

00:00:24.529 --> 00:00:29.480
by multiplying two 13 digit numbers together
in her head.

00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:34.420
And she was able to give the answer in 28
seconds, and that was just the amount of time

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:37.840
it took for her to say the answer out loud.

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:42.790
There are some clips of her on YouTube performing
math shows where she answers questions from

00:00:42.790 --> 00:00:48.050
the audience in her head much faster than
her scribe can even write them down on the

00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:49.050
chalkboard.

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:53.620
Devi: "Mr. Sherma, you want the 8th root of
this number?

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:56.560
7 is the answer right.

00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:00.680
I can even give you the steps.

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:05.390
7 multiplied by - Yogesh you're not writing
anything!

00:01:05.390 --> 00:01:07.159
Please go ahead.

00:01:07.159 --> 00:01:17.760
7 multiplied by 7 is 49, multiplied by 7 is
343, multiplied by 7 is 2401, multiplied by

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:24.610
7 is 16807, multiplied by 7 is - oh you don't
want to do anymore?

00:01:24.610 --> 00:01:27.150
Ok then well I will do it anyway for my satisfaction
- multiplied by 7 is 117649, multiplied by

00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:32.500
7 is 823543 and that multiplied by 7 is the
answer you wrote on the blackboard."

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:38.300
She loved doing multiplications, finding roots
of numbers, or even figuring what day of the

00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:40.470
week a certain date fell on.

00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:41.900
Devi: "25th April 1960?

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:45.880
That's a Monday"

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:51.310
And these are all amazing but what really
amazed me also is just how funny she was.

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:54.190
Devi: "Will you compete with me?

00:01:54.190 --> 00:02:04.360
Oh goodness that's no use, I mean you can't
get approximate numbers it's got to be exact.

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:06.940
The machines have their limitations whereas
humans have no limitations."

00:02:06.940 --> 00:02:12.530
She had confidence and made jokes and was
really good at putting on a show.

00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:17.170
And that's not something that's always so
common for someone with these abilities.

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:19.569
Which actually does have a name by the way,
hypercalculia.

00:02:19.569 --> 00:02:26.370
Her charisma on stage might make a bit more
sense when you understand a bit about her

00:02:26.370 --> 00:02:27.370
background.

00:02:27.370 --> 00:02:28.990
Devi was from India and her.

00:02:28.990 --> 00:02:30.700
father was a circus performer.

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:36.980
It was him who discovered her ability in mathematics
while he was teaching her a card trick when

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:39.130
she was only 3 years old.

00:02:39.130 --> 00:02:46.570
After then he started taking her on math road
shows and she went on to impress and entertain

00:02:46.570 --> 00:02:48.970
people all over the world.

00:02:48.970 --> 00:02:53.130
And she did all of that without having any
formal education.

00:02:53.130 --> 00:02:57.920
When I see what she was able to do, my question
is 'how?'

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:04.290
And luckily she did write a book explaining
some of her methods and I have that book her

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:05.290
today.

00:03:05.290 --> 00:03:10.480
It is called Figuring and I would like to
go through it with you and show you some of

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:11.670
her explanations.

00:03:11.670 --> 00:03:17.880
In her introduction Devi says that she has
a desire to show those who think mathematics

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:21.210
is boring just how beautiful it can be.

00:03:21.210 --> 00:03:27.690
And she says to understand the methods describes
you need nothing more than a basic knowledge

00:03:27.690 --> 00:03:28.950
of arithmetic.

00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:34.860
She then has a chapter on the digits where
she explains each number one by one and the

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:36.880
patterns that can be seen with it.

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:42.700
She refers to something called secret steps
in multiplication where after doing a normal

00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:49.319
multiplication and being left with a 2 digit
number, she will add up those two digits to

00:03:49.319 --> 00:03:51.819
get left with a single number.

00:03:51.819 --> 00:03:57.870
For the 1 times tables this last digit will
follow the pattern that it increases by 1

00:03:57.870 --> 00:03:59.070
each time.

00:03:59.070 --> 00:04:02.400
Something similar can be seen in the 2 times
table.

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:07.990
There are likewise patterns in the secret
steps of the 3 times tables, 4 times tables,

00:04:07.990 --> 00:04:10.680
and all of the digits really.

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:16.810
And there's always some kind of pattern that
she manages to find in these remaining digits.

00:04:16.810 --> 00:04:23.380
These patterns give rules to working out multiplications
and I guess ways to check your answers, and

00:04:23.380 --> 00:04:30.389
show how she played with numbers to such an
extent that she could find patterns everywhere.

00:04:30.389 --> 00:04:34.610
Since multiplication was what she had the
world record for, we'll take a little look

00:04:34.610 --> 00:04:37.520
at some of her methods for multiplication.

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:43.030
She starts with just multiplying two 2-digit
numbers, 63 and 48.

00:04:43.030 --> 00:04:45.250
And she gives in order in which it should
be done.

00:04:45.250 --> 00:04:49.670
We start here on the right and do this column
which is 3x8.

00:04:49.670 --> 00:04:57.740
It's 24 so you should put a 4 down and carry
the 2 over to our next one.

00:04:57.740 --> 00:05:07.590
Here we will add up the products of the diagonals
so we'll do 4x3 plus 6x8 plus that remaining

00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:09.250
2 that we were carrying.

00:05:09.250 --> 00:05:12.280
That will give us 62.

00:05:12.280 --> 00:05:15.330
So write the 2 down and carry the 6.

00:05:15.330 --> 00:05:23.150
For our last one we'll do 4x6 plus the 6 that
we carried and that will be 30.

00:05:23.150 --> 00:05:28.230
So by the end of that we'll have the answer
3024.

00:05:28.230 --> 00:05:36.330
And this method can be extended to 3-digit
numbers, 4-digit numbers and all the way up

00:05:36.330 --> 00:05:37.330
really.

00:05:37.330 --> 00:05:41.380
She gives an example here of multiplying two
6-digit numbers this way.

00:05:41.380 --> 00:05:46.020
It takes a few extra steps and she acknowledges
that most people would want to do this with

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:50.820
a pen and paper but in the end she does get
the correct product.

00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:56.820
How she manages to do it so fast is still
beyond me even though I've read some of her

00:05:56.820 --> 00:05:57.820
methods.

00:05:57.820 --> 00:06:04.800
But maybe it's a combination of raw talent,
a lot of practice, and spending time to notice

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:10.760
relationships between numbers to speed up
each step of the process.

00:06:10.760 --> 00:06:15.789
Something else that she did a lot of on stage
was finding square roots, so let’s take

00:06:15.789 --> 00:06:18.400
a little look at one of her methods there.

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:22.430
She says that first you’ll want to learn
a table of squares of numbers from one to

00:06:22.430 --> 00:06:24.860
nine off by heart.

00:06:24.860 --> 00:06:29.950
And from this you can see that a square ending
in a one would have a root ending in either

00:06:29.950 --> 00:06:32.699
a one or a nine.

00:06:32.699 --> 00:06:38.710
And a square ending in a four would have a
root ending in either a two or eight.

00:06:38.710 --> 00:06:46.130
Squares ending in a nine or a six will also
have two possible root endings, and a square

00:06:46.130 --> 00:06:52.789
ending in five will only have one possibility
which is that the root also ends in five.

00:06:52.789 --> 00:06:58.690
Now to use this table to find square roots,
say that we have a four digit number: 6, 2,

00:06:58.690 --> 00:06:59.690
4, 1.

00:06:59.690 --> 00:07:04.780
We’ll break that down into 62 and 41.

00:07:04.780 --> 00:07:10.810
Take the 62 and find where it lies on the
table.

00:07:10.810 --> 00:07:18.370
It is less than 64 but more than 49, so the
highest possible first digit of the root would

00:07:18.370 --> 00:07:20.000
be a seven.

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:26.030
Now we move on to using 41 to try and find
the units digit of the square root.

00:07:26.030 --> 00:07:32.111
We know that since it ends in a one, that
the root must end in a nine or a one, and

00:07:32.111 --> 00:07:38.699
to decide which one to use, what we do is
we take the first number of the root, in this

00:07:38.699 --> 00:07:44.150
case seven, and we multiply it by itself plus
one.

00:07:44.150 --> 00:07:46.160
So multiply it by seven plus one.

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:50.270
That’ll be seven times eight equal to 56.

00:07:50.270 --> 00:07:57.289
And since 56 is less than 62, which is what
we used to find that first number of the root,

00:07:57.289 --> 00:08:04.300
then we will take the highest of the two possible
digits figures, so in this case it will be

00:08:04.300 --> 00:08:05.319
a nine.

00:08:05.319 --> 00:08:13.271
If 56 had been higher than 62, then we would’ve
taken the lower of the two possible digit

00:08:13.271 --> 00:08:15.330
figures and that would’ve been a one.

00:08:15.330 --> 00:08:22.280
So the two digits we worked out are seven
and nine, to give the root of 6241 as indeed

00:08:22.280 --> 00:08:23.280
79.

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:28.060
There’s a few seemingly random steps there
but what we did is we broke our number down

00:08:28.060 --> 00:08:34.329
into two, we used the first half of it to
find the first digit, and we used the ending

00:08:34.329 --> 00:08:38.749
of the second half of it to find out what
our root should end in.

00:08:38.749 --> 00:08:44.290
To do larger squares, you will need to remember
a bit more of the table, and of course this

00:08:44.290 --> 00:08:47.230
goes on for even larger numbers still.

00:08:47.230 --> 00:08:52.790
And there’s a pretty similar method for
how she works out cube roots and roots of

00:08:52.790 --> 00:08:55.230
higher powers as well.

00:08:55.230 --> 00:08:59.350
And there’s one more fun method that she
talks about here and this one’s probably

00:08:59.350 --> 00:09:01.550
the easiest to understand.

00:09:01.550 --> 00:09:06.769
It is how she works out what day of the week
any date will fall on.

00:09:06.769 --> 00:09:10.259
She says that you’ll need to memorise four
tables.

00:09:10.259 --> 00:09:13.959
Table one, which is a few multiples of seven.

00:09:13.959 --> 00:09:19.990
The second, which corresponds each month to
a digit.

00:09:19.990 --> 00:09:25.910
And the third table which takes each fourth
year, maybe these are leap years, I’m not

00:09:25.910 --> 00:09:28.980
sure, and corresponds them to a digit as well.

00:09:28.980 --> 00:09:32.629
And this table repeats, so you only really
need to memorise a section of it.

00:09:32.629 --> 00:09:37.600
And there’s a fourth table here which will
turn the result of our calculation which will

00:09:37.600 --> 00:09:42.589
be a number from zero to six, into a corresponding
day of the week.

00:09:42.589 --> 00:09:49.569
So if we wanted to work out what day of the
week was the 9th of April 1932, the first

00:09:49.569 --> 00:09:55.130
thing that we do is reduce the day of the
month to a number less than seven.

00:09:55.130 --> 00:10:00.629
And to reduce a number to something less than
seven, we would need to subtract any of these

00:10:00.629 --> 00:10:04.379
four numbers from table one, depending on
how big it is.

00:10:04.379 --> 00:10:10.319
In this case, we would do nine minus seven,
and be left with two.

00:10:10.319 --> 00:10:15.269
We then use table two and find out that April
corresponds to a six.

00:10:15.269 --> 00:10:19.579
This is added to the two, so we now have eight.

00:10:19.579 --> 00:10:25.329
We always want to remain lower than seven
since there are seven days in the week, so

00:10:25.329 --> 00:10:29.119
we will subtract seven and be left with a
one.

00:10:29.119 --> 00:10:35.509
Now we’ll take a look at the year, 1932,
and we’ll find from this table three that

00:10:35.509 --> 00:10:38.100
it corresponds to a five.

00:10:38.100 --> 00:10:45.199
Since 1932 was one of the years on the table,
all we need to do is add the digit from previously

00:10:45.199 --> 00:10:50.549
which is a one, so five plus one is equal
to six, and we can just use table four to

00:10:50.549 --> 00:10:56.339
find out what day of the week six corresponds
to, and that will be a Saturday.

00:10:56.339 --> 00:11:01.769
There are slightly different rules if the
year doesn’t fall exactly on that table

00:11:01.769 --> 00:11:08.089
of leap years, but it would just involve finding
the previous leap year plus the difference

00:11:08.089 --> 00:11:11.129
between that leap year and the year in question.

00:11:11.129 --> 00:11:15.920
Again, a few seemingly random steps here,
especially when I just read them out.

00:11:15.920 --> 00:11:21.220
But with a little bit of practise, this method
becomes something kind of routine, you just

00:11:21.220 --> 00:11:27.060
need to memorise those tables and these few
steps, and you can work out any day of the

00:11:27.060 --> 00:11:28.720
week you want.

00:11:28.720 --> 00:11:33.259
She talks about some of her methods to check
her answer such as this one that she refers

00:11:33.259 --> 00:11:39.420
to as casting out the nines, because even
a human computer will occasionally make mistakes,

00:11:39.420 --> 00:11:44.810
but she’s also able to check things in her
mind so it means it’s much less likely a

00:11:44.810 --> 00:11:46.690
mistake will slip through.

00:11:46.690 --> 00:11:50.379
To finish I'd like to take a little look at
her conclusion.

00:11:50.379 --> 00:11:55.509
She says that mathematical discoveries, like
all others, may come from the need to solve

00:11:55.509 --> 00:11:56.810
practical problems.

00:11:56.810 --> 00:12:03.360
But a more important motive is the pure delight
some individuals take in invention and discovery.

00:12:03.360 --> 00:12:10.839
I’ve seen a little bit of criticism online
in referring to Shakuntala Devi as a mathematician,

00:12:10.839 --> 00:12:16.949
since what she does is really just mental
arithmetic, some would call it, but I would

00:12:16.949 --> 00:12:22.480
say she deserves to be called a mathematician
because of her outlook on what mathematics

00:12:22.480 --> 00:12:23.480
is.

00:12:23.480 --> 00:12:29.369
A lot of these methods in this book she comes
up with herself by spending hours in the company

00:12:29.369 --> 00:12:34.769
of numbers, thinking about the patterns between
them and thinking about ways to group them

00:12:34.769 --> 00:12:40.309
together, different ways to solve the problem,
and finding various tricks that will work.

00:12:40.309 --> 00:12:46.759
But I think all of that does come down to
the nature of mathematics which is experimentation

00:12:46.759 --> 00:12:49.930
and finding delight in numbers.

00:12:49.930 --> 00:12:54.699
She says the intent of this book is that she
hopes the readers will come to share this

00:12:54.699 --> 00:12:59.769
feeling for the subject, and so I hope by
showing you a little bit through it in this

00:12:59.769 --> 00:13:04.040
video, you’ve gained a bit of that appreciation.

00:13:04.040 --> 00:13:09.109
Thanks for watching to the end of the video,
and a big thanks to my Patreon supporters

00:13:09.109 --> 00:13:12.859
who are the people who make these videos possible
in the first place.

00:13:12.859 --> 00:13:18.980
And a shoutout to today’s patron cat of
the day, Toth, who doesn’t look too much

00:13:18.980 --> 00:13:21.789
like a cat but still gets a few minutes of
fame.

